l\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.accounts import Account\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import Direction, JournalEntry, Posting\nfrom pypara.accounting.ledger import Ledger, LedgerEntry\n```\n\nWait \u2014 is `Direction` importable from `pypara.accounting.journaling`? The get_info for Direction failed, but that might be because the tool couldn't find it by that name. In pypara, `Direction` is defined in `pypara/accounting/journaling.py`:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    DBT = 1\n    DCR = -1\n```\n\nYes, I'm fairly confident. The `Posting` dataclass has a `direction: Direction` field, and `Direction` must be imported in journaling.py. So `from pypara.accounting.journaling import Direction` should work.\n\nAlso `Amount` from `pypara.commons.numbers` \u2014 `Amount = NewType('Amount', Decimal)`.\n\nTest structure:\n\n```python\nclass TestLedger:\n    def _make_account(self):\n        ...\n    def _make_posting(self, amount, direction):\n        ...\n```\n\nTest 1: `_last_balance` with empty entries returns `initial.value` (covers line 130).\n\n```python\ndef test_last_balance_empty_returns_initial(self):\n    initial_value = Quantity(Decimal(\"100\"))\n    ledger = Ledger(account=self.account, initial=Balance(datetime.date(2020, 1, 1), initial_value))\n    assert ledger._last_balance == initial_value\n    assert ledger.entries == []\n```\n\nTest 2: `add` on empty ledger (covers lines 140, 143, 146, and 128 via the second add... wait, no).\n\nActually, when `add` is called on an empty ledger, `_last_balance` raises IndexError \u2192 line 130 covered. Then entry created (line 140), appended (143), returned (146).\n\nTest 3: `add` on non-empty ledger \u2192 `_last_balance` returns `entries[-1].balance` (line 128 covered).\n\nSo:\n\n```python\ndef test_add_to_empty_ledger(self):\n    initial = Balance(datetime.date(2020, 1, 1), Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n    ledger = Ledger(self.account, initial)\n    posting = self._make_posting(Decimal(\"50\"), Direction.DBT)\n    entry = ledger.add(posting)\n    assert isinstance(entry, LedgerEntry)\n    assert entry.ledger is ledger\n    assert entry.posting is posting\n    assert entry.balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"150\"))\n    assert ledger.entries == [entry]\n    # last balance now comes from the entry\n    assert ledger._last_balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"150\"))\n\ndef test_add_to_nonempty_ledger(self):\n    ...\n    first = ledger.add(posting1)  # 100 + 50 = 150\n    second = ledger.add(posting2)  # 150 + 30 = 180 (debit) or 150 - 30 = 120 (credit)\n    assert second.balance == ...\n    assert ledger.entries == [first, second]\n    assert ledger._last_balance == second.balance\n```\n\nAlso test credit direction: `posting.amount * posting.direction.value` with DCR (-1) subtracts.\n\nLet me also verify the balance computation: `Quantity(self._last_balance + posting.amount * posting.direction.value)`.\n\n- Initial 100, debit 50 \u2192 100 + 50*1 = 150.\n- Then credit 30 \u2192 150 + 30*(-1) = 120.\n\nNow about cleanup / state pollution: The `Ledger` objects are created fresh in each test, so no global state is mutated. `JournalEntry` and `Posting` are frozen dataclasses. No monkeypatching needed since we don't touch module-level state. But the instructions say \"Be sure to clean up after the test... use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since we don't modify any global state, no cleanup is strictly needed \u2014 each test creates its own objects. I could add a fixture that creates fresh objects per test, which naturally isolates state. I'll use a fixture or just helper methods creating fresh instances per test. That's clean.\n\nHmm, but should I use monkeypatch for something? Not really necessary. The guidance says \"if appropriate\". Creating fresh objects per test is sufficient. Maybe I can note that no global state is touched. I'll just make sure everything is constructed within each test.\n\nLet me write the account stub:\n\n```python\nclass _StubAccount:\n    \"\"\"Minimal stub satisfying the Account protocol.\"\"\"\n    @property\n    def code(self):\n        return \"100\"\n    @property\n    def name(self):\n        return \"Cash\"\n    @property\n    def type(self):\n        return None\n    @property\n    def coa(self):\n        return None\n    @property\n    def parent(self):\n        return None\n```\n\nHmm, `AccountType` \u2014 I couldn't get info. I'll just return None for type/coa/parent since the tested code never uses them. Actually, to be safer, maybe use `Mock(spec=Account)`. Since `Account` is runtime_checkable Protocol, `Mock(spec=Account)` works and provides all attributes. But `spec` on a Protocol class... `Mock(spec=Account)` \u2014 creating a Mock with spec of a Protocol class can be tricky because Protocol classes have abstract properties; Mock handles it fine generally (it uses `dir()` on the spec). Actually for runtime_checkable protocols, `dir(Account)` includes the properties. `Mock(spec=Account)` should work. But there can be issues with `_is_protocol` attributes. Let me just use a simple stub class \u2014 simplest and most robust.\n\nActually, even simpler: since the Ledger never uses the account in the tested code paths, I could pass a `Mock()`. But a stub is more explicit and doesn't require mock. I'll go with a stub.\n\nFor the journal entry: `JournalEntry(date, description, source)`. Source can be None.\n\nLet me now also double check `Quantity` and `Amount` are NewTypes of Decimal \u2014 arithmetic works directly on Decimal. `Quantity(Decimal(\"100\"))` \u2014 NewType just returns the value. So `Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")) == Decimal(\"100\")` is True. Good.\n\nThe `add` method: `Quantity(self._last_balance + posting.amount * posting.direction.value)`. `posting.amount` is an `Amount` (Decimal), `posting.direction.value` is int (1 or -1). So the arithmetic is Decimal arithmetic. Good.\n\nNow, one subtlety: `Balance` is frozen dataclass with `date: datetime.date` and `value: Quantity`. Constructor: `Balance(date=..., value=...)`.\n\nLedger constructor: `Ledger(account=..., initial=...)`. `entries` has `init=False` with default_factory=list.\n\nNow let me write the full test file:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for pypara.accounting.ledger.Ledger covering previously uncovered lines.\"\"\"\n\nimport datetime\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.accounts import Account\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import Direction, JournalEntry, Posting\nfrom pypara.accounting.ledger import Ledger, LedgerEntry\n\n\nclass StubAccount:\n    \"\"\"\n    Minimal stub satisfying the ``Account`` protocol; only identity is relevant\n    for the ledger model under test.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, code: str = \"100\"):\n        self._code = code\n\n    @property\n    def code(self) -> str:\n        return self._code\n\n    @property\n    def name(self) -> str:\n        return \"Cash\"\n\n    @property\n    def type(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def coa(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def parent(self):\n        return None\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef account() -> StubAccount:\n    \"\"\"Provides a fresh stub account for each test (no shared state).\"\"\"\n    return StubAccount()\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef initial_balance() -> Balance:\n    \"\"\"Provides a fresh initial balance for each test.\"\"\"\n    return Balance(date=datetime.date(2020, 1, 1), value=Decimal(\"100\"))\n\n\ndef _make_posting(account, amount: Decimal, direction: Direction) -> Posting:\n    \"\"\"Creates a fresh posting bound to its own journal entry.\"\"\"\n    journal = JournalEntry(date=datetime.date(2020, 1, 2), description=\"Test entry\", source=None)\n    return Posting(journal=journal, date=datetime.date(2020, 1, 2), account=account, direction=direction, amount=amount)\n\n\nclass TestLedgerLastBalance:\n    def test_last_balance_of_empty_ledger_falls_back_to_initial(self, account, initial_balance):\n        ledger = Ledger(account=account, initial=initial_balance)\n        assert ledger.entries == []\n        assert ledger._last_balance == Decimal(\"100\")\n\n    def test_last_balance_of_non_empty_ledger_is_last_entry_balance(self, account, initial_balance):\n        ledger = Ledger(account=account, initial=initial_balance)\n        first = ledger.add(_make_posting(account, Decimal(\"50\"), Direction.DBT))\n        assert ledger._last_balance == first.balance == Decimal(\"150\")\n\n\nclass TestLedgerAdd:\n    def test_add_to_empty_ledger_debit(self, account, initial_balance):\n        ledger = Ledger(account=account, initial=initial_balance)\n        posting = _make_posting(account, Decimal(\"50\"), Direction.DBT)\n\n        entry = ledger.add(posting)\n\n        assert isinstance(entry, LedgerEntry)\n        assert entry.ledger is ledger\n        assert entry.posting is posting\n        assert entry.balance == Decimal(\"150\")  # 100 + 50 * (+1)\n        assert ledger.entries == [entry]\n        assert ledger._last_balance == entry.balance\n\n    def test_add_to_non_empty_ledger_credit(self, account, initial_balance):\n        ledger = Ledger(account=account, initial=initial_balance)\n        first = ledger.add(_make_posting(account, Decimal(\"50\"), Direction.DBT))\n        second_posting = _make_posting(account, Decimal(\"30\"), Direction.DCR)\n\n        second = ledger.add(second_posting)\n\n        assert isinstance(second, LedgerEntry)\n        assert second.ledger is ledger\n        assert second.posting is second_posting\n        assert second.balance == Decimal(\"120\")  # 150 + 30 * (-1)\n        assert ledger.entries == [first, second]\n        assert ledger._last_balance == second.balance == Decimal(\"120\")\n\n    def test_add_returns_new_entry_each_time_and_appends_in_order(self, account, initial_balance):\n        ledger = Ledger(account=account, initial=initial_balance)\n        e1 = ledger.add(_make_posting(account, Decimal(\"10\"), Direction.DBT))\n        e2 = ledger.add(_make_posting(account, Decimal(\"20\"), Direction.DBT))\n        e3 = ledger.add(_make_posting(account, Decimal(\"5\"), Direction.DCR))\n\n        assert [e.balance for e in ledger.entries] == [Decimal(\"110\"), Decimal(\"130\"), Decimal(\"125\")]\n        assert ledger._last_balance == Decimal(\"125\")\n        assert e1 is not e2 is not e3  # hmm, chained comparison: e1 is not e2 and e2 is not e3\n```\n\nWait, `e1 is not e2 is not e3` \u2014 chained comparison means `e1 is not e2 and e2 is not e3`. That's fine but a bit obscure; I'll write it more clearly or drop it.\n\nAlso, should I verify `entry.date`, `entry.is_debit` etc.? Those are LedgerEntry properties not in the uncovered lines; not needed. Keep tests focused.\n\nOne concern: does `LedgerEntry` get exported from `pypara.accounting.ledger`? Yes, it's defined in the same module (per the get_info result showing it in ledger.py context \u2014 actually the get_info output didn't specify the module for LedgerEntry, but the imports in the snippet show `from pypara.accounting.ledger import ...`? Actually the snippet shows imports of Account, Balance, Posting, Quantity, and LedgerEntry is referenced in the Ledger class. The get_info for LedgerEntry showed a dataclass with `ledger: 'Ledger[_T]'` \u2014 forward reference to Ledger, so it must be in the same module `pypara/accounting/ledger.py`. Good, so `from pypara.accounting.ledger import Ledger, LedgerEntry` works.\n\nAnother concern: `Direction` import. The get_info failed for \"Direction\" \u2014 maybe the tool indexes only certain names. In pypara's journaling.py, the actual code is:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    DBT = 1\n    DCR = -1\n```\n\nI'm quite confident about this from the pypara