tity` \u2014 the ledger code wraps results in `Quantity(...)`. For assertions, comparing `entry.balance == Decimal(\"150\")` works since Quantity is just Decimal. But to be faithful, I'll wrap expected values in `Quantity(...)` too. Either way works. I'll use Quantity for clarity.\n\nNow, about \"clean up after the test to avoid state pollution\": All objects are local to each test; fixtures create fresh instances. No module-level mutable state is touched. No monkeypatch needed. I might add a comment noting this. The instruction says use monkeypatch/pytest-mock \"if appropriate\" \u2014 it's not needed here since nothing global is patched. Fresh fixtures per test provide isolation.\n\nOne more consideration: should I test `_last_balance` directly (private attribute)? The uncovered lines include 127-130 which is the `_last_balance` property. Testing it directly via `ledger._last_balance` is the most direct way to cover both branches. The `add` tests also cover it indirectly. I'll include direct tests.\n\nAlso, edge: `test_last_balance_of_empty_ledger` covers line 130 (except branch). `test_add_to_empty_ledger` also triggers line 130 (via add \u2192 _last_balance on empty ledger) plus 140/143/146. `test_add_to_non_empty` covers line 128.\n\nLet me also make sure `Ledger` is Generic \u2014 `Ledger(account=..., initial=...)` fine.\n\nLet me also double-check `JournalEntry` constructor: fields `date`, `description`, `source`, then `postings` (init=False), `guid` (init=False). So `JournalEntry(datetime.date(2020,1,2), \"Test entry\", None)` works.\n\nAnd `Posting(journal, date, account, direction, amount)` \u2014 all positional or keyword. Frozen dataclass, so keyword init fine.\n\nNow write the final file. Keep top-level code minimal: imports, stub class, fixtures, helper, test classes/functions. No `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` or pytest.main.\n\nLet me also think about whether `entries` comparison `ledger.entries == [entry]` works \u2014 LedgerEntry is a dataclass with eq=True by default, comparing ledger (identity), posting (frozen dataclass eq), balance (Decimal). `JournalEntry` contains `postings` list and `guid` \u2014 eq compares all fields; two postings from different journals won't be equal, but we compare the same objects, so fine. Actually `ledger.entries == [entry]` compares the same entry object to itself \u2014 dataclass eq compares field by field: `ledger is ledger` True, `posting is posting` True, balance equal. Fine.\n\nAlternatively use `len(ledger.entries) == 1 and ledger.entries[0] is entry`. I'll do both-ish: assert `ledger.entries == [entry]` is fine.\n\nLet me finalize:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nTests for :mod:`pypara.accounting.ledger`, specifically covering the previously\nuncovered lines of ``Ledger._last_balance`` and ``Ledger.add``.\n\nEach test builds its own object graph via fixtures, so no global state is\ntouched and no cleanup/patching is required to avoid state pollution.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport datetime\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.accounts import Account\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import Amount, Direction, JournalEntry, Posting, Quantity\nfrom pypara.accounting.ledger import Ledger, LedgerEntry\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `Amount` and `Quantity` are in `pypara.commons.numbers`, not journaling. journaling imports them but re-export via `from pypara.commons.numbers import Amount, Quantity` \u2014 importing from journaling would work since it's imported there, but cleaner to import from commons.numbers. I'll do:\n\n```python\nfrom pypara.commons.numbers import Amount, Quantity\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import Direction, JournalEntry, Posting\n```\n\nStub account: to satisfy the Protocol at type-check level, properties. Fine.\n\nFull code:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nTests for :mod:`pypara.accounting.ledger`.\n\nThese tests specifically target the previously uncovered lines of\n``Ledger._last_balance`` (both the populated and the empty-ledger branches)\nand ``Ledger.add`` (entry creation, buffering and return).\n\nEvery test builds its own object graph through fixtures, so no global or\nmodule-level state is mutated and no patching/cleanup is necessary.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport datetime\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import Direction, JournalEntry, Posting\nfrom pypara.accounting.ledger import Ledger, LedgerEntry\nfrom pypara.commons.numbers import Amount, Quantity\n\n\nclass StubAccount:\n    \"\"\"\n    Minimal stub satisfying the ``Account`` protocol. The ledger model under\n    test only stores the account, so hard-coded property values suffice.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @property\n    def code(self):\n        return \"100\"\n\n    @property\n    def name(self):\n        return \"Cash\"\n\n    @property\n    def type(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def coa(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def parent(self):\n        return None\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef account():\n    \"\"\"A fresh stub account per test (no shared state between tests).\"\"\"\n    return StubAccount()\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef initial():\n    \"\"\"A fresh initial balance per test.\"\"\"\n    return Balance(date=datetime.date(2020, 1, 1), value=Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n\n\ndef make_posting(account: StubAccount, amount: Amount, direction: Direction) -> Posting:\n    \"\"\"Builds a posting bound to its own dedicated journal entry.\"\"\"\n    journal = JournalEntry(date=datetime.date(2020, 1, 2), description=\"Test journal entry\", source=None)\n    return Posting(\n        journal=journal,\n        date=datetime.date(2020, 1, 2),\n        account=account,\n        direction=direction,\n        amount=amount,\n    )\n\n\nclass TestLastBalance:\n    \"\"\"Covers ``Ledger._last_balance`` (lines 127-130).\"\"\"\n\n    def test_empty_ledger_falls_back_to_initial_value(self, account, initial):\n 